**Proposition 107.2 (an earned operand really used).** After either
cold $d=0$ repair at $BH\theta$, compute $q=\theta_1/2$ from the
acquired data ($q=z_1$ in the fresh run, $q=-y_1$ in the inherited run).
Actually submit Add$(p_1LR,q)$;Hold;Read$(p_1LR)$. The complete scalar
reply and real successor are

$$
\operatorname{reply}=q,\qquad
w_+=BH\theta+q e_{p_1LR}=G BH\theta,\qquad
G=I+e_{p_1LR}e_{p_1R}^{\mathsf T},\quad
G^{-1}=I-e_{p_1LR}e_{p_1R}^{\mathsf T}.
\tag{107.2}
$$

This adds one Add, one Hold and one Read. The same selected use works
after partial-advice repair once the full parameter has been earned.

**Proof.** At $BH\theta$ the designated sensor is zero and
$(BH\theta)_{p_1R}=q$. Add therefore gives exactly the displayed
reply and field. The two distinct physical coordinates make the
rank-one part of $G$ square to zero, proving its inverse. All $p_iR$
coordinates are unchanged, so they still decode $\theta$. The predictor
is updated with this actual operand and successor. It then maintains
the whole original service through arbitrary later legal calls by
Theorem 64.1, without asserting that these calls preserve a historical
chart. $\square$

